 "literal": "Like nectar, beautiful sweet speech / such a sweet woman's qualities also are like this / if they cause painful, intense distress / living together is difficult indeed; love / avoid approaching it, O wise people!",
 "translation": "Her words are sweet, beautiful, and like nectar. If the qualities of such a sweet woman also cause this kind of painful, intense distress, then living together in love is indeed difficult. O wise people, avoid approaching love!",
 "modern": "அமிர்தம் போன்ற இனிமையான சொற்களைப் பேசும் அந்தப் பெண்ணின் குணங்களும் இத்தகைய துன்பத்தைத் தருவதாக இருந்தால், அவளோடு கூடி வாழ்வது மிகவும் அரிதானது. அறிவுடையவர்களே, நீங்கள் காமத்தை நெருங்குவதைத் தவிர்த்துவிடுங்கள்.",
 "explanation": "In this Kuriñci poem, the hero expresses his deep anguish caused by love and separation. He addresses wise people, warning them to stay away from love. He describes his beloved as a sweet woman whose words are as delightful as nectar, yet her qualities now bring him intense, painful distress. He laments that living together in love is extremely difficult when it is accompanied by such suffering. The contrast between the nectar-like sweetness of her words and the agonizing pain of separation highlights the overwhelming power of love.",
